WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
A chain of amino acids folds itself into one precise shape.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.920
Doesn't the second law say disorder always wins?

3
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.640
Watch the water around it.

4
00:00:14.017 --> 00:00:16.217
The second law counts total entropy.

5
00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:21.717
That means the protein, the water around it, and the heat they trade with their

6
00:00:21.717 --> 00:00:24.697
surroundings. So here is the rule.

7
00:00:26.077 --> 00:00:27.877
Count the water, not just the molecule.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.920
Start with one oily group, a methyl on an alanine side chain.

9
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.340
Water cannot hydrogen bond to it.

10
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.500
So the water touching it points its bonds along the surface.

11
00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.440
That layer has fewer ways to arrange itself: lower entropy.

12
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.500
Pictures often draw this layer as an ice cage.

13
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:51.860
That overstates it.

14
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.820
The layer is only slightly stiffer than bulk water.

15
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.140
But a protein has many oily groups.

16
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.100
Around all of them, it adds up.

17
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:06.270
Now gather the scattered oily groups into one cluster.

18
00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.290
What happens to the water's entropy?

19
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.410
Up, or down? Decide before the picture moves.

20
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:25.263
It goes up. One cluster has less surface than six scattered groups.

21
00:01:26.023 --> 00:01:28.083
So less water must line it.

22
00:01:29.143 --> 00:01:30.943
The freed water rejoins the bulk.

23
00:01:32.163 --> 00:01:36.683
The groups gather mainly because the water gains freedom, not because they attract.

24
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:40.427
That release is the hydrophobic effect.

25
00:01:41.707 --> 00:01:44.907
Near room temperature, its gain is almost all entropy.

26
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:49.307
At higher temperatures, enthalpy takes a bigger share.

27
00:01:50.547 --> 00:01:52.707
It does at large, flat surfaces too.

28
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:59.110
Fold a small protein—about a hundred residues—using round numbers near room

29
00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:05.670
temperature. The chain's lost freedom costs about plus +550 kJ/mol.

30
00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.950
Freed water gives back minus −500.

31
00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:13.850
Bonds and packing inside the fold add about minus −90.

32
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.040
The total is about minus −40.

33
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.960
Folding is favored, by a small difference between big numbers.

34
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.200
Shift the burial term by ten percent.

35
00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.600
The total moves by more than its own size.

36
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:33.180
That is why one bad mutation can unfold a protein.

37
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.600
And the second law?

38
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.160
The water's entropy gain covers most of the chain's loss, but not quite all.

39
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.120
The bonds inside release heat.

40
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.760
That heat raises the entropy of the surroundings.

41
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.560
Count everything, and total entropy rises.

42
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:54.810
So the second law is not broken.

43
00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.410
And order is not free.

44
00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.130
In this ledger, water pays most of the bill.

45
00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.250
It wins back its freedom when oily surfaces hide.
